icanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-20T17:15:25Z", "editorId": "Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct·cloud·GH#27878163626", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Dispute between the austrian habsburg monarchy and an alliance of saxony and prussia Took place in Bohemia. Date: 1778. Ended: 1779-05-13.", "newValue": "The War of the Bavarian Succession, occurring in 1778-1779, was a pivotal dispute between the Austrian Habsburg Monarchy and an alliance of Saxony and Prussia, ultimately taking place in Bohemia. This conflict marked a significant turning point in the complex web of European alliances and territorial claims, as the Habsburgs sought to consolidate their power and secure their hold on the region. At the heart of the conflict lay the election of a new Bavarian ruler, which would have far-reaching consequences for the balance of power in Central Europe.

The War of the Bavarian Succession was a critical moment in the complex dance of European diplomacy, with the great powers of the time - Austria, Prussia, and Saxony - vying for influence and control. The conflict was sparked by the death of the Elector of Bavaria and the subsequent election of a new ruler, Charles Theodore, who was supported by the Habsburgs. The war itself was a series of skirmishes and battles, with the Prussian-Saxon alliance ultimately emerging victorious. The Treaty of Teschen, signed in 1779, marked the end of the conflict and established the new borders of Bavaria.

